<a id="features"></a>
## Features

- 🧲 **Works on destroyed filesystems** — reads the raw byte stream, no mount, no wallet app.
- 💽 **Multiple inputs** — a `dd`/`ddrescue` image **file**, a raw **block device** (`/dev/sdX`),
  a **directory / mounted filesystem**, or **all mounted filesystems** at once.
- 🔑 **Broad coverage** — WIF, `xprv`/`xpub`, Bitcoin Core `wallet.dat`, Electrum 1.x + 2.x,
  BIP39, standalone seed files, and DER EC keys.
- 🔓 **Password testing** — feeds your known-password list to every encrypted wallet it finds,
  using the exact Bitcoin Core and Electrum key-derivation functions.
- ✅ **No false positives** — every reported hit is cryptographically/structurally validated.
- 💰 **Balance checking** — derives addresses and (optionally) queries on-chain balances, with
  an OPSEC-safe "addresses only" mode for your own node.
- 📟 **Live feedback** — a single self-updating progress line plus instant on-screen alerts the
  moment a wallet is recovered.
- 🧩 **Self-contained** — pure Python + `cryptography` (+ optional `mnemonic`/`electrum`).

---

<a id="supported-wallets-and-seed-types"></a>
## Supported wallets and seed types

| Scheme | What it recovers |
|---|---|
| `wif` | WIF private keys (base58check) |
| `der-key` | DER-encoded EC private keys from `wallet.dat`/disk — **pywallet `--recover`** (both 214-byte compressed and 279-byte uncompressed layouts) |
| `ext` | Extended keys `xprv` / `xpub` / `tprv` / `tpub` |
| `core-mkey` | Bitcoin Core `wallet.dat` master key + encrypted keys — **incl. password testing** |
| `electrum-v4` | Pre-2.0 Electrum wallet (`seed_version 4`, 2011–2014) — **incl. password testing** |
| `electrum-2x-plain` | Electrum 2.x wallet stored in plaintext (JSON) |
| `electrum-bie1` | Electrum 2.x encrypted wallet (`BIE1`/ECIES) — **incl. password testing** |
| `mnemonic-file` | A standalone file whose **entire** content is ONLY a seed phrase (BIP39 **or any Electrum type**) |
| `bip39` | BIP39 seed phrase embedded anywhere inside larger text |
| `hex` | raw 64-hex private-key candidates (**unverified**, not in `all`) |
| `privkey-sweep` | 32-byte sliding-window sweep, balance-filtered (for small carved regions; needs `--check-balance`) |

**Electrum coverage — all versions:**

| Era | Seed form | Detection | Addresses |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1.x (old) | 32-hex seed / 1626-word mnemonic | hex in wallet, or mnemonic with `electrum` installed | its own derivation (uncompressed P2PKH) |
| 2.0+ `standard` (0x01) | word phrase | `HMAC("Seed version")` prefix | `m/0/i`, `m/1/i` → P2PKH |
| 2.0+ `segwit` (0x100) | word phrase | `HMAC("Seed version")` prefix | `m/0'/0/i`, `m/0'/1/i` → P2WPKH |
| 2.0+ `2fa` (0x101) / `2fa_segwit` (0x102) | word phrase | `HMAC("Seed version")` prefix | detected only (TrustedCoin cosigner needed — open in Electrum) |

---

<a id="installation"></a>
## Installation

Ubuntu/Debian. A dedicated **virtualenv** is recommended so nothing touches the system Python.

```bash
# 1) system packages
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y python3 python3-venv python3-pip gddrescue

# 2) create and activate a virtualenv
python3 -m venv ~/wallet-rescue-venv
source ~/wallet-rescue-venv/bin/activate

# 3) install dependencies
pip install --upgrade pip
pip install cryptography mnemonic
```

| Package | Needed for |
|---|---|
| `cryptography` | **required** — AES-CBC for every decryption |
| `mnemonic` | BIP39 checksum validation (BIP39 schemes skipped without it; Electrum does **not** need it) |
| `electrum` | **optional** — enables detection of **old Electrum 1.x mnemonics** (ships the 1626-word list) |

### Old Electrum (1.x) seed support

Old Electrum wallets used their own 1626-word list. To detect a standalone **old** Electrum
mnemonic, install Electrum into the same venv (it's used only as a word-list source):

```bash
source ~/wallet-rescue-venv/bin/activate
pip install electrum
```

No network / can't install Electrum? Point the tool at the word list instead:

```bash
python wallet_rescue.py -i disk.img -s electrum-v4,mnemonic-file \
  --electrum-old-wordlist /path/to/english_old.txt -o out
```

### Verify the install

```bash
python wallet_rescue.py --selftest     # checks all crypto primitives against known test vectors
# -> must print:  selftest: PASS
```

> Prefer no virtualenv? `python3 wallet_rescue.py --install-deps -i …` installs the packages
> itself (and falls back to `--break-system-packages` on Ubuntu's externally-managed Python).

---

<a id="how-to-use"></a>
## How to use

A typical end-to-end recovery:

```bash
# 1) image the source drive (never write to the original)
sudo ddrescue -d -r3 /dev/sdX disk.img disk.mapfile

# 2) put your known passwords in a file, one per line
printf '%s\n' 'myOldPass1' 'hunter2' '123456' > password_list.txt

# 3) scan everything, test passwords, check balances
python wallet_rescue.py -i disk.img -p password_list.txt -s '*' --check-balance -o rescue.out
```

What you get:
- Recovered wallets/keys/seeds printed **immediately** on screen as they are found.
- `rescue.out/findings.jsonl` — every hit, written live (mode `0600`).
- `rescue.out/addresses.txt` and `rescue.out/balances.txt` — derived addresses and on-chain balances.

### "Find everything" — one command

```bash
python wallet_rescue.py -i disk.img -p password_list.txt -s '*' --check-balance -o rescue.out
```

`-s '*'` enables **every** detector (including the unverified `hex`). Use `-s all` to get all
**validated** schemes but skip `hex`.

---

<a id="input-modes"></a>
## Input modes

| Mode | Command | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Disk image file | `-i disk.img` | `dd`/`ddrescue` image; also works on partial images with gaps |
| Raw block device | `sudo -i /dev/sdX` | sized via `lseek`, mmapped demand-paged (no full RAM load) — needs root |
| Directory / mounted FS | `-i /mnt/olddrive` | recursive, file by file; findings include the **file path** |
| All mounted filesystems | `--scan-mounts` | every real mount from `/proc/mounts` (pseudo-fs skipped) |

Filesystem scans stay on a single filesystem by default (like `find -xdev`); add
`--cross-device` to cross mount points, and `--max-file-mb N` to skip very large files.

```bash
# scan one mounted drive
python wallet_rescue.py -i /mnt/olddrive -s all --check-balance -o rescue.out

# scan the whole running system (as root)
sudo python wallet_rescue.py --scan-mounts -s all --max-file-mb 2048 -o rescue.out
```

---

<a id="creating-a-disk-image"></a>
## Creating a disk image

Image the source device **first** and never write to the original drive. `ddrescue` is more
robust on failing disks and can resume via its mapfile:

```bash
# preferred: ddrescue (skips read errors, mapfile allows resuming)
sudo ddrescue -d -r3 /dev/sdX disk.img disk.mapfile

# simple alternative with dd
sudo dd if=/dev/sdX of=disk.img bs=4M conv=noerror,sync status=progress
```

Replace `/dev/sdX` with the **source drive**. `wallet-rescue` can also scan `/dev/sdX`
directly, but a read-only image is safer for a failing drive and lets you re-run without the
disk attached.

---

<a id="scheme-selection"></a>
## Scheme selection

`-s` / `--schemes` takes a comma list of: exact id · wildcard · group · `all` · `*`

```bash
-s all                     # all validated schemes (excludes hex)
-s '*'                     # literally everything (includes hex)
-s 'electrum-*'            # every Electrum scheme
-s electrum-v4,core-mkey   # just these two
-s der-key                 # only the pywallet-style DER key recovery
-s mnemonic-file           # only standalone seed files
```

List them any time:

```bash
python wallet_rescue.py --list-schemes
```

---

<a id="options"></a>
## Options

| Option | Effect |
|---|---|
| `-i FILE` | image file, block device, or directory to scan |
| `--scan-mounts` | scan every mounted real filesystem (instead of `-i`) |
| `-p FILE` | password list (one per line, UTF-8) |
| `-o DIR` | output directory (default `carver_out`) |
| `-s LIST` | scheme selection (default `all`) |
| `--check-balance` | derive addresses **and** query on-chain balance |
| `--addresses-only` | derive addresses, **no** API query (OPSEC-safe) |
| `--api-url URL` | Esplora-compatible API (default `https://blockstream.info/api`) |
| `--gap N` | address gap limit for seed/xprv derivation (default 20) |
| `--hex` | additionally run the unverified `hex` scheme |
| `--seedfile-nochecksum` | `mnemonic-file`: also report exact 12/24-word regions with a **failing** checksum |
| `--electrum-old-wordlist FILE` | 1626-word list for old Electrum mnemonics |
| `--min-iter N` / `--max-iter N` | sanity window for Bitcoin Core `mkey` iteration counts |
| `--sweep-max-mb N` | `privkey-sweep`: max input size (default 0.25 MB) |
| `--sweep-step N` | `privkey-sweep`: byte step between candidates (default 1) |
| `--max-file-mb N` | filesystem mode: skip files larger than N MB (0 = no limit) |
| `--cross-device` | filesystem mode: descend across mount points |
| `--no-progress` | disable the progress line |
| `--install-deps` | install Python dependencies via pip |
| `--list-schemes` | print available schemes |
| `--selftest` | verify crypto primitives against test vectors |

---

<a id="checking-balances-and-privacy"></a>
## Checking balances and privacy

```bash
# OPSEC-safe: derive addresses only, check them yourself on your own node
python wallet_rescue.py -i disk.img -s all --addresses-only -o out
#   -> out/addresses.txt

# query balances (this discloses your addresses to the API server!)
python wallet_rescue.py -i disk.img -s all --check-balance -o out
#   -> out/balances.txt   (funded addresses shown live, ">>>" marks a balance)
```

> **Privacy note:** `--check-balance` reveals the queried addresses to the API operator. For
> maximum privacy use `--addresses-only` with your own node, or point `--api-url` at your own
> Esplora/mempool.space-compatible instance.

Addresses are derived per hit type: private keys → P2PKH (compressed/uncompressed), P2WPKH,
P2SH-P2WPKH; old Electrum → its own derivation; Electrum 2.x → standard/segwit paths; and
`xprv`/`xpub`/BIP39 → BIP44/49/84 + Electrum paths up to the gap limit.

---

<a id="output-files"></a>
## Output files

Everything lands in the `-o` directory (created `0700`):

| File | Content |
|---|---|
| `findings.jsonl` | one JSON object per hit, written live (`0600`); private keys include compressed + uncompressed **WIF** |
| `addresses.txt` | derived addresses (with `--check-balance` / `--addresses-only`) |
| `balances.txt` | used / funded addresses with balances (with `--check-balance`) |

`status` values: `VALID` / `DECRYPTED` / `PLAINTEXT` = confirmed hit ·
`ENCRYPTED` = found but no password matched · `UNVERIFIED` = hex candidate with no guarantee.

---

<a id="how-it-works"></a>
## How it works

`wallet-rescue` finds candidates by signature, then **proves** each one before reporting it:

- **WIF / xprv** — base58check (deterministic, zero false positives).
- **Bitcoin Core `mkey`** — the correct password decrypts the master key to a full PKCS7 pad
  block `0x10`×16 (random chance ≈ 256⁻¹⁶). The same KDF as Bitcoin Core (`BytesToKeySHA512AES`).
- **DER EC keys** — require the exact `30 81 D3`/`30 82 01 13 … 04 20 … A0 81 85 30 81 82`
  layout and cross-check the embedded public key.
- **Electrum `BIE1`** — the HMAC-SHA256 over the ECIES blob **is** the password check.
- **Old Electrum seed** — decrypts to exactly 32 hex characters (PKCS7-checked).
- **BIP39** — valid checksum; `mnemonic-file` additionally requires the region to contain
  **only** the seed (kills README/source-code false positives).
- **Electrum 2.x** — the canonical `HMAC("Seed version")` version prefix.
- **privkey-sweep** — every 32-byte window is a technically valid key, so it is filtered purely
  by on-chain balance (no false positives by construction).

Scanning is chunked (64 MiB with overlap) so it streams multi-terabyte inputs without loading
them into RAM, and hits that straddle chunk boundaries are never lost.

---

<a id="troubleshooting"></a>
## Troubleshooting

- **`externally-managed-environment` on pip install** → use a venv (above) or `--install-deps`.
- **`SyntaxError` on startup** → run with **python**, not `bash`: `python wallet_rescue.py …`.
- **`cannot read /dev/sdX`** → raw block devices need root: run with `sudo`.
- **API 403 / rate limiting** → use your own node via `--api-url`, or `--addresses-only`.
- **BIP39 schemes skipped** → `pip install mnemonic` (Electrum seeds don't need it).
- **Old Electrum mnemonic not detected** → `pip install electrum` or pass `--electrum-old-wordlist`.
- **Scan seems stuck** → it's working; the single progress line shows %, MB/s and ETA. Large
  disks simply take a while.

